Why not Apache-2.0
  proto/api.proto is copied verbatim from iTerm2 (https://github.com/gnachman/iTerm2), which is
  distributed under the GPL. A copy of that file cannot be relicensed by us, and Apache-2.0 is not
  compatible with GPL-2.0-only. Replacing the file with a clean-room protocol definition would be
  the only route to a permissive license; that has not been done.

Why "or later" (checked 2026-11-01)
  - iTerm2's own `iterm2` Python package, the direct equivalent of this library, declares
    "GPL v2 or later" (https://pypi.org/project/iterm2/).
  - iTerm2's LICENSE file is the GPL v2 text and GitHub reports it as GPL-2.0; its README says
    GPLv3. "GPL-2.0-or-later" is consistent with all three: a combined work can be distributed
    under GPLv3, which also keeps Apache-2.0 dependencies legal.
  - Unverified: iTerm2 carries no per-file license header on api.proto.

